step D: evaluating
triage: fix_tests: In all three failing scenarios the game applied the rules correctly to the moves it actually received: the fee was paid, the drop payment made, the tie-breaks by fewer mice and then seat order worked, and the sack was valued correctly. The tests' scripted bids (for example 3 for seat 1) were never applied, every stayer fell back to the default minimum bid of 1, and the expected mice, cat_points and winner_seat were all computed from bids that were never placed.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: fix_tests: Step 11 reveals every remaining sack card, and step 14 discards the whole sack face up, so after a round has fully finished every placed card has been shown. The game's revealed_cards holding the whole sack (3 of 3, 5 of 5, 4 of 4) is consistent with the rules; the R6 and R11 tests read that list after the round ended, when only the first and second reveals had been counted. In R13 both stayers bid 1, which looks like the scripted bids of 3 and 5 never matched a bid the game offered, so the game fell back to the minimum. That points at how the test scripted its moves, not at the winner logic: with equal bids of 1 the tie-break correctly gave seat 1 the sack.

